<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanford B. Dole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sanford Ballard Dole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanford B. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a> was a Hawaiian-born lawyer of American missionary parents who led the government formed after the overthrow of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliuokalani</a> in 1893. He was president of the Provisional Government and from 1894 of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, pressing for annexation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, achieved in 1898, and became the first governor of the Territory of Hawaii in 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole.jpg" alt="Sanford B. Dole" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanford_B._Dole_holding_newspaper.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sanford Ballard Dole was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> on 23 April 1844 to Protestant missionaries from Maine. His father, Daniel Dole, was principal of Oahu College, and his mother, Emily Hoyt Ballard, died within days of his birth. The family moved to Kōloa on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> in 1855, where they ran another school that Sanford attended. He studied at Oahu College and at Williams College in 1866–1867, and read law in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> office. Elected to the legislature of the Hawaiian Kingdom for Kauaʻi in 1884 and 1886, he served until 1887 and was active in securing the constitution of 1887, which he and other lawyers of American descent drafted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The 1887 constitution, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>, limited voting to literate males of Hawaiian, European and American descent and set income and wealth requirements for voting for the House of Nobles. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a> to the Supreme Court in December 1887, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> to her Privy Council in 1891. After the coup that ended the monarchy on 17 January 1893 he helped draft the Committee of Safety's declaration, though he did not join it, and became president of the Provisional Government, which was recognised within 48 hours by all nations with diplomatic ties to the kingdom.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a>'s commissioner James Henderson Blount reported in July 1893 that the Committee of Safety had conspired with the US ambassador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> to land marines and remove the queen. The Morgan Report of February 1894 concluded instead that the overthrow was locally based and that the troops had only protected American property and citizens. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> was proclaimed on 4 July 1894, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a>, elected its first and only elected president, served until 1898. Voting rights were restricted, which kept power with residents of American and European descent. He put down the counter-rebellion of January 1895 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-william-wilcox">Robert William Wilcox</a>, and commuted the death sentences of its leaders.</p><p>In January and February 1898 he visited Washington to urge annexation. Congress passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newlands-resolution">Newlands Resolution</a> on 4 July 1898, effective 12 August, and the transfer was marked that day at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a>. In June 1900 he became the first governor of the Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, resigning in November 1903 to serve as a federal district judge until his retirement on 16 December 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a> died on 9 June 1926 after a series of strokes, and his ashes were placed in the cemetery of Kawaiahaʻo Church. Dole Middle School in Kalihi Valley was named after him in 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanford B. Dole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sanford B. Dole</b> headed an executive council, with an advisory council of the overthrow's supporters, and ruled without elections, requiring officials to swear loyalty.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-hawaii">Provisional Government of Hawaii</a></li><li><blockquote>The new constitution was proclaimed on 4 July 1894, creating a republic with <b>Sanford B. Dole</b> as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-hawaii">Republic of Hawaii</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sanford B. Dole</b>, president of the republic, became the first territorial governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-hawaii">Territory of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1897" aria-label="Honolulu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="309 1376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sanford B. Dole"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.31,-157.86&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
